# Component helpers

How a component is built in this codebase: a `render_<component>`
helper is the public entry point, and the partial is pure markup. New
and converted components follow this pattern. (`button` is the
reference implementation; some older components are still plain
partials, mid-migration.)

## The shape

```erb
<%%= render_button(variant: "primary") do %>
  Save changes
<%% end %>
```

Three files per component:

1. **Helper** — `app/helpers/gesso/components/<name>_helper.rb`,
   `module Gesso::Components::<Name>Helper`, public method
   `render_<name>(...)`. The single entry point callers use; never
   `render "gesso/components/<name>"` directly.
2. **Partial** — `app/views/gesso/components/_<name>.html.erb`. Pure markup.
3. **Preview** — `spec/components/previews/<name>_preview.rb` plus a
   `<name>_preview/` template dir.

## The helper owns the logic

- Allowed enum values as frozen constants (`VARIANTS`, `SIZES`).
- **Validation:** raise `ArgumentError` naming the allowed values for
  an unknown enum option — fail fast at the call site.
- Class composition, resolving options to the values basecoat
  expects, defaults, and any value objects.
- Build markup with **Rails tag helpers** (`button_tag`), not
  hand-written HTML; suppress unwanted defaults (`name: nil`) and join
  classes with `class_names`.
- Call the partial with the **string-plus-block** render form
  (`render("gesso/components/<name>", locals, &block)`); the explicit
  `partial:`/`locals:` form does not pass the block to `yield`.

```ruby
module Components
  module ButtonHelper
    VARIANTS = %w[ default primary secondary outline ghost link
                   destructive warning info ].freeze
    SIZES = %w[ default xs sm lg icon icon-xs icon-sm icon-lg ].freeze

    def render_button(variant: "primary", size: "default", **opts, &block)
      unless VARIANTS.include?(variant)
        raise ArgumentError, "variant must be one of #{VARIANTS.join(", ")}"
      end
      # ... compose locals, then:
      render("gesso/components/button",
        variant: basecoat_value(variant), size: basecoat_value(size),
        **opts, &block)
    end
  end
end
```

## The partial is pure markup

- A strict `locals: (...)` signature on the first line —
  receiving ready-made values (e.g. a `variant:` already resolved to
  what basecoat expects), not raw options.
- No `case`, no validation, no class maths — that all lives in the
  helper.
- Accept `classes`; `yield` the block for content.

## Previews use a template

Inline helper calls fail in a preview — Lookbook/ViewComponent defers
the preview `render`. Use `render_with_template` with a shared template
that calls the helper (the helper is auto-included in the template's
view context — no `extend`):

```ruby
def primary
  render_with_template(template: "button_preview/preview",
    locals: { variant: "primary", label: "Primary" })
end
```

Add a `variants` scenario rendering every variant together, for the
doc page to embed.

## Specs

- **Helper unit spec** — `spec/components/<name>_helper_spec.rb`,
  `type: :helper`. Call the helper directly in each example
  (`helper.render_<name>(...) { label }` wrapped in `Capybara.string`)
  and assert on the HTML. Do not abstract setup into a shared
  `def`/proc — keep each example self-contained.
- **Request spec** — `spec/components/<name>_spec.rb`, `type: :request`,
  still renders `/lookbook/preview/<name>/<scenario>` and asserts DOM.

## Doc page

`spec/components/docs/05_components/<name>.md.erb`:

- A **Usage** section with syntax-highlighted ` ```erb ` blocks showing
  the `render_<name>` call (basic + with common options).
- When the component has variants or modes: a table listing them, an
  embedded gallery (`<%%= embed :<name>, :variants %>` — an embed, not
  raw HTML, so the iframe loads only the app CSS and avoids the
  `lookbook.css` preflight leak), and concrete **when to use / when not
  to use** guidance for each.
